  "intro": "The U.S. exported **$21.82B** of articles of iron or steel in 2025, down **5.1%** from $23.00B in 2024 after four straight years of growth. Two headings make up more than half: other articles such as forgings, stampings and fabricated parts (7326, **29%**, $6.41B) and screws, bolts, nuts and washers (7318, **26%**, $5.63B). Mexico and Canada take **60%** between them, much of it parts and fasteners for factories on the other side of the border. In the first eight months of 2026 exports were up 2.2%, with Mexico up 7.5% and Canada down 9.4%.\n\nTariffs matter most in **Canada**: since September 8, 2026 it charges a 50% surtax on nearly every U.S. steel line of the chapter (25% from March 13, 2025), with no exemption for goods that qualify for CUSMA. **China** adds its 10% extra duty to rates of 3–8%. Elsewhere tariffs have fallen or stayed at zero: the EU cut its duties on U.S. steel articles to zero on July 1, 2026, and Mexico, South Korea, Singapore and Australia charge nothing on qualifying goods under U.S. free-trade agreements. For pipe and tube the limits are steel quotas instead: the EU and the UK charge 50% once their quotas are used up.",
